---
Compilerpedia -- the Botlish compiler's built-in reference (offline)

What Botlish means, what it guarantees, what this compiler can prove, and why
its rules and warnings exist: every diagnostic code, warning, language rule,
proof capability, standard-library symbol and backend fact is a topic with a
stable ID, reachable from several indices and by search.

BEFORE YOU WRITE A PROGRAM
    Botlish looks like Python. These habits fail to compile, or compile and
    mean something else:

        You might write                Botlish
        for x in xs:                   loop x in xs:
        for i in range(n):             loop i from 0 to n:
        while c:                       loop:  (its body ends it with break)
        v = a if c else b, c ? a : b   v = if c:  (each branch on its own line)
        if c: x  (on one line)         x on the next line, indented
        else if                        elif
        x = x + 1, x += 1              a new name; or recurse, passing x + 1
        total = total + x  in a loop   compiles; binds a new total in the loop
                                       body, and the outer total never changes:
                                       fold x in xs with intermediate total 0:
        r = ... in each if branch,     UNBOUND after the if (a branch's names
        then r after the if            end with it): write r = if c:
        f(x, y)  your own function     x.f(y), or declare it nomethod fn
        "x"  one character             'x'

    The whole page, with a checked program: botlish -help primer

New to Botlish?  botlish -help "best practices"

INDICES
    concepts    What Botlish's constructs are and why they exist (100)
    rules       Language rules and semantic guarantees (38)
    proofs      What the compiler proves, and what it does not (15)
    errors      Diagnostic codes and the specific rules behind them (258)
    warnings    The linter's rules, with their rationale (10)
    lint        The linter as a whole: policy, modes and principles (12)
    principles  Programming principles and recommended practices (17)
    stdlib      Standard-library modules, functions and types (717)
    backends    Backends, targets, the command line and platforms (53)
    all         every topic ID, by index

LOOKING THINGS UP
    botlish -help KNOWN-ERROR              a code (error or warning)
    botlish -help std::list::at            a standard-library symbol
    botlish -help concept.method-sugar     a topic ID
    botlish -help "bounds proof"           an alias, or a word search
    botlish -help "handle several errors"  a task
    botlish -help "negative tests"         testing a failing call
    botlish -help warnings                 an index

    An exact topic ID, code or symbol wins over everything else, then an
    alias, then the topics that contain every word of the query. Case does not
    matter, except that a code's last word in capitals (TYPE, STRUCT) is the
    code and in lower case a word. Several matches are listed best first.

