rule.package-modules
Installed packages: .bot_modules, downward only
Indices: rules

SUMMARY
    A package is a module tree in its owner's .bot_modules/NAME, claiming the
    root namespace NAME there; it sees its own modules, its own packages and
    std only, and each copy is its own instance.

THE RULE
    A package is an ordinary module tree installed under the immediate
    `.bot_modules` directory of its owner: the application's package NAME is
    ROOT/.bot_modules/NAME/ (ROOT the entry file's directory), and that
    package's own package D is ROOT/.bot_modules/NAME/.bot_modules/D/. The
    directories are the whole of it: no manifest, version or registry is read.
    The package manager (cli.packages) builds such trees from botlish.json
    and .botpkg artifacts; the compiler reads only the directories, and a
    hand-assembled tree compiles the same.

        game/main.bot                      import sdl3::video
        game/.bot_modules/sdl3/sdl3/video.bot
        game/.bot_modules/sdl3/.bot_modules/helper/helper.bot

    For an import a file writes, by the namespace's first segment:

      * `std::...` is the standard library's, for every file;
      * a segment naming a package installed in the file's own scope (its
        .bot_modules/NAME is a directory) is that package's: `NAME` is
        PKG/NAME.bot and `NAME::a` PKG/NAME/a.bot. The package claims the
        whole root namespace there: an own file of that namespace beside it is
        MODULE-COLLISION, never a precedence;
      * otherwise the application's file ROOT/NAME.bot, or, in a package, its
        own root namespace's module (PKG/NAME.bot, PKG/NAME/a.bot) -- a
        package's own modules are its root namespace's only.

    Nothing is looked up upward or sideways: a package never sees its
    consumer's modules or packages, a sibling package or an ancestor's, and the
    application never sees a package its package installed. Imports stay exact
    and non-transitive, and a leading `::` never escapes a scope.

TARGET PACKAGE TREES
    When ROOT/.bot_modules holds a directory .targets, the application's
    packages are the tree of the compilation's target (-target, the host's by
    default), ROOT/.bot_modules/.targets/TARGET -- an ordinary package tree in
    every other respect -- so packages whose bindings or native files differ by
    target are compiled from the right ones, and compiling for one target never
    reads another's. A .targets without the target's tree gives the application
    no packages for that target (UNKNOWN-NAMESPACE says so). The package
    manager derives these trees (cli.packages: target views); a HIR compiled
    from one is its target's, and the tree's .lib directory is searched when
    the program links natively.

IDENTITY
    A package instance is its place in the dependency tree (`physics`, the
    application's; `physics/geometry`, physics's own geometry). A module's
    identity is that place and its namespace below the package's root:
    `sdl3::video` for the application's sdl3, `physics/geometry::shapes` for a
    module of physics's geometry, which no source can spell. Two installed
    copies of a package are two instances: physics/geometry::Point and
    rendering/geometry::Point are distinct types, their errors distinct
    errors, on every backend.

WHY
    A package must work wherever it is installed, so it resolves only what it
    installed itself; and an installation never changes the meaning of a
    program it is not part of (BOT-MODULES-MILESTONE-1.md). A package needs a
    directory and files, so there is no single-file example.

RELATED
    rule.application-modules    import std::NAME is the standard library's
                                stdlib/NAME.bot (or intrinsics); any other
                                import NAME is the file NAME.bot beside the
                                entry program; nothing is searched and nothing
                                falls back.
    concept.modules             A module is a source file loaded by `import`,
                                its namespace its path; it holds declarations
                                and immutable values, and a program's own
                                modules are the files beside its entry file.
    concept.imports             `import NS` allows NS::member names and makes
                                NS's functions method candidates; `import type
