---
cli.packages
The package manager: botlish.json, .botpkg artifacts, add and install
Indices: backends

SUMMARY
    botlish add, install, update, remove and verify manage a project's
    packages from local .botpkg artifacts and botlish.json; package pack makes
    an artifact. Nothing is downloaded or run.

THE COMMANDS
        botlish add NAME|FILE.botpkg|DIR [-request R] [-replace]
        botlish install
        botlish update [NAME...]
        botlish remove NAME...
        botlish verify
        botlish package pack [DIR] [-out DIR] [-force]
        botlish package inspect FILE.botpkg [-target T]
        botlish native inspect FILE [-target T]
        botlish native plan [-target T] [-include]
        botlish native deploy -target T -out DIR [-force]
    (tclsh9.0 main.tcl add ... in a checkout.) add, install, update, remove
    and verify take -project DIR (the project: the directory of its
    botlish.json; default the current directory) and -repository DIR (a local
    repository of NAME-VERSION.botpkg files; any number, never searched
    implicitly); the first four also -force (replace installed packages that
    were changed), -recover (roll back an interrupted operation), -target T
    (prepare the target view of T) and -drop-target T (remove it); verify
    -target T verifies that view's native dependencies again.

WHAT THEY DO
      * add NAME installs the newest version a repository holds (or the newest
        -request accepts), writes the request (MAJOR.MINOR by default) and the
        exact lock to botlish.json, and installs the package's own locked
        dependencies in its own .bot_modules. add FILE.botpkg records the
        archive and its SHA-256 as the dependency's source; add DIR (a path
        with "/") links an editable directory, which has no lock.
      * install recreates exactly what botlish.json locks, from the
        repositories; it never changes a lock. update moves locks to the newest
        version their requests accept (1.2 never becomes 1.3); remove deletes a
        dependency and its installed tree.
      * verify compares installed files with the installation record and,
        where a repository holds them, with the artifacts' bytes.
      * package pack makes a deterministic NAME-VERSION.botpkg from a package
        directory whose botlish.json has a name, a version and a lock for
        every dependency, checking a target-aware package's layout and every
        bundled native file first.

TARGETS AND NATIVE DEPENDENCIES
    A botlish.json of schema 2 may name the targets a package supports
    (`targets`): for each, a directory of target-specific files (generated
    bindings, native archives, shared libraries) and its native libraries --
    bundled (a file of the package) or system (the target's own installation),
    static or shared, with the facts the manager checks. One release (one name
    and version, one SHA-256) holds every target it names. When an installed
    package is target-aware, the manager derives a target view per prepared
    target, .bot_modules/.targets/TARGET: every package with that target's
    files at their module paths, and the bundled libraries in its .lib. The
    compiler reads the view of the target it compiles for (rule.package-modules)
    and links that .lib without -L; a native backend's run finds its shared
    libraries there. Preparing another target never touches a view already
    made; a package that does not name a target is UNSUPPORTED for it.
      * native inspect reads a native file (ELF, ar, Mach-O, PE/COFF) without
        running it; native plan shows a view's native closure -- what links,
        from where, verified how, and what must be deployed (-include prints
        the packages' public header directories as -I options); native deploy
        places a target's bundled shared libraries in DIR/lib (Linux, macOS)
        or DIR (Windows), as a transaction recorded in DIR, for an executable
        linked with -runtime-path '$ORIGIN/lib' (cli.options).
      * A system library is verified only on its own target's host, by the
        system's metadata (its dpkg package or a pkg-config record), never by a
        file merely present; for another target it is reported unverified.
      * One process has one native loader namespace: two packages bundling
        different libraries of one name, a bundled and a system one, one
        loader identity for two libraries or two static archives defining one
        symbol are refused, never resolved by a choice. botlish.json's
        "origins" chooses system or bundled where a package offers both.

WHAT THEY NEVER DO
    Download anything, run anything a package holds (no install scripts,
    hooks or builds), touch a .bot_modules entry they did not install, replace
    a changed installed package without -force, or claim more than integrity:
    local artifacts carry no publisher signature. Every operation is one
    transaction: a failure, or an interruption the next command recovers,
    leaves the previous installation as it was.

THE COMPILER
    The compiler reads the .bot_modules trees the package manager builds
    exactly as hand-made ones (rule.package-modules), and never reads
    botlish.json, a version, a lock, an artifact or native
    metadata. A package's dependencies
    are in its own .bot_modules, so the application cannot import them;
    two packages may lock different versions of one dependency, and each
    gets its own instance.

RELATED
    rule.package-modules        A package is a module tree in its owner's
                                .bot_modules/NAME, claiming the root namespace
                                NAME there; it sees its own modules, its own
                                packages and std only, and each copy is its
                                own instance.
    concept.modules             A module is a source file loaded by `import`,
                                its namespace its path; it holds declarations
                                and immutable values, and a program's own
                                modules are the files beside its entry file.
    concept.native-link-plan    `extern c library "NAME" static` requires a
                                static archive, `static at "PATH"` names its
                                file from the application root, and `extern c
                                framework "NAME"` links an Apple framework.
    cli.usage                   botlish [OPTION...] FILE... compiles and runs
                                each file; with no arguments it prints the
                                usage summary; -help opens Compilerpedia;
                                -version prints the build identity.
    cli.exit-status             botlish exits 0 when every input compiled and
                                ran, 1 when any failed, 2 for a malformed
                                invocation; -help exits 1 when nothing
                                matches; an executable exits 0 or, after a
                                run-time error, nonzero.
    backend.targets             -target NAME picks the operating system and
                                architecture a program is built for (C type
                                sizes, modules, platforms); native code runs
                                only for the host's own target.

